aight draw (a Queen would give you a straight) and a backdoor flush draw (one club in hand, two on board). Your opponent raised pre-flop but checked the flop, which often indicates they missed the high cards (like the King) or are exercising pot control. While betting could take down the pot immediately, your hand has showdown value that benefits from keeping the pot small. Betting risks a check-raise from a slow-played King or strong draw, which would force you to fold your equity.\n\nReason: Checking allows you to see the next card for free, potentially improving your hand to a straight or two pair, while avoiding the risk of getting raised off your middle pair. Since you are currently in the lead for the overall game, a low-variance play that realizes your equity is optimal.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
